      "raw": "<p>I think it's exactly the other way around.&nbsp; Had they done RPG books 6 years ago, it would have been at the expense of SotM, which was massively successful.&nbsp; That would have sunk them.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Now, if they'd launched the RPG 3 years ago, instead of OblivAeon?&nbsp; Maybe.&nbsp; OblivAeon took way too long and my understanding is that it was primarily Spirit Island that kept them afloat while the core GtG team worked on OblivAeon.&nbsp; But also 3 years ago wasn't the right time for the product.&nbsp; Not only did we need the story of OblivAeon for the setting to make sense, I don't think the market for RPG's was in a good place then.&nbsp; It was saturated with 5th Edition D&amp;D and indies trying to compete with it.&nbsp; IMHO there's more breathing room now.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I also don't think we'll see more than one book a year... but that's a good thing!&nbsp; You don't want to flood your base with too many purchases in too short a time.&nbsp; You do need to allow for proper proofreading, playtesting, and art development and direction.&nbsp; Companies that push out too many cheap books too fast (looking at you, Green Ronin) publish a lot that's trash.</p>",
